      <h2>Current Agent Training Uses “Flood Irrigation”</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-height:min(76vh,620px)">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Problem in Existing Paradigms</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Trajectory imitation injects full demonstrations regardless of need.</li>
            <li>Trial-and-error approaches add reflection everywhere, not selectively.</li>
            <li>Knowledge-augmented methods query knowledge in all steps (Know%=100%).</li>
            <li>Result: unnecessary context, higher compute, and potential action noise.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Why This Fails</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Different states require different cognition budgets.</li>
            <li>Humans switch between intuitive, deliberative, and knowledge-seeking modes.</li>
            <li>Agents lacking metacognitive routing overuse expensive modules.</li>
            <li>Goal: situationally decide <span class="highlight">when not</span> to retrieve knowledge.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="footer-note">Core thesis: indiscriminate knowledge injection is both wasteful and suboptimal.</div>
      <div class="nav">2 / 8</div>
